        \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${lim}_{x\\to 2}g(x)=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-1$\n\nCurrent part:\n\nFind ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }g(x)$ and ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }g(x).$ If a limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }g(x)=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                                \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }g(x)=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "On the left side of the graph, we see that as the values of $x$ decrease and approach $-\\infty ,$ the graph of the function $g(x)$ moves closer and closer to the horizontal asymptote $y=3.$\n\nConsequently, $g(x)$ approaches the value $3$ as $x$ approaches $-\\infty ,$ and we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }g(x)=3.\n$$\n\nOn the right side of the graph, as the values of $x$ increase and approach $\\infty ,$ the graph of the function decrease without bound to $-\\infty .$   Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }g(x)=-\\infty .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186245042 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b94d5612-763f-59d8-9a80-7f6e2f18fa6c" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Constant Rule to Compute a Limit Algebraically"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b8b393a3-03e3-5120-8cfb-6e0479140586" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245044}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b8b393a3-03e3-5120-8cfb-6e0479140586" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245044} :answer-field/id #uuid "19dbbc6d-a3fc-57af-a2d9-e182df3cff4d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245043}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c70454df-a8a4-5cee-bf50-c27bbb42d751" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-131480" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13932295/assets/e489437aa3b22a90d71dea3d30ff7a53bb800580775666f97dd4a6cf2db76e6d.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graphs of the functions $y=f(x)$ and $y=g(x).$\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\nFind ${lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{-}}g(x)$ and ${lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{+}}g(x).$ If a limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{-}}g(x)=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $2$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                                \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{+}}g(x)=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $0$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\nFind ${lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)}g(x).$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)}g(x)=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        DNE\n\nEarlier part 3:\n\nFind ${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}g(x).$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${lim}_{x\\to 2}g(x)=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-1$\n\nEarlier part 4:\n\nFind ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }g(x)$ and ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }g(x).$ If a limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }g(x)=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $3$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                                \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }g(x)=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-\\infty$\n\nCurrent part:\n\nGiven that ${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}Cf(x)=\\frac{4}{3},$ where $C$ is a real constant, what is the value of $C?$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $C=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "From the graph, we see ${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)=4.$\n\nWe factor the constant out of the limit and evaluate the remaining limit, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 2}Cf(x) & =\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ C\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 2}f(x) & =\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ C\\cdot 4 & =\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ C & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186245045 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "38e6d549-0d5a-56e3-88b0-0b22dceb2d0d" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Sum Rule to Compute A Limit Given a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27881758-ae1e-5e40-8fa3-c1bfd79502e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245047}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "27881758-ae1e-5e40-8fa3-c1bfd79502e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245047} :answer-field/id #uuid "a1cf3e6d-60c1-5377-9b6e-65a58602d1de" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245046}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "89bd35d8-8c70-5a18-8dc1-54b8d0ae57a0" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-131481" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13932295/assets/e489437aa3b22a90d71dea3d30ff7a53bb800580775666f97dd4a6cf2db76e6d.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graphs of the functions $y=f(x)$ and $y=g(x).$\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\nFind ${lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{-}}g(x)$ and ${lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{+}}g(x).$ If a limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                  